arehouse  at the school system headquarters  because the school system says they  never asked for the books and they think  they're of not high enough quality to be  used for instruction so these books just  sort of showed up when the score says  school system's doorstep one day he  unasked-for unsolicited but the books  are good for lots of laughs it seems it  is hard to believe whose books were  bought purely on the base of literary  merit reporters who've read them have  pointed out that for a start she can't  seem to settle on the correct spelling  of the name for one of her main  character educational children's book  about eating right she spell vegetable  rock we do know the mayor had some books  in her house and her car never got to  any kids as saw during the FBI raid they  were carrying out many of the boxes of  the healthy Holly books before she  resigned mayor Pugh had offered an  apology to the people of Baltimore for  anything unethical  you want to say that I apologize that  done something to upset the people the  people of Baltimore and I love care  about and then she launched into a  bizarre three-minute pitch for her new  line of baby clothing for the son it has  been a story no other news outlet has  come close to matching was one of those  times when everybody in the newsroom  knows there's a big story so it's sort  of an all-hands-on-deck approach I mean  I think at different points probably  maybe 10 or more reporters have had a  byline on this story and serves as yet  another example of the value of robust  local reporting Lee I think like this  shows the Sun what the Sun at its best  and what the Sun can do yeah we we we've  we have covered something that nobody  knew was happening we exposed it and  there's been swift change and  ramifications that would never have  happened if it were not for a strong  local newspaper so the FBI investigation  continues in Baltimore as does the  reporting of the Baltimore Sun and Luke  Broadwater next a shout out from  students earnest who watched as their  fellow classmates were murdered and then  went to work as reporters when we return  we close out this special edition with a  shout out for all the reporters who won  Pulitzer Prizes this year and for a  group that did not win the Pulitzer  Prize but for whom the Pulitzer judges  went out of their way to honor right  before I announce who they are I want to  break with tradition and offer my  sincere admiration for an entry that did  not win but that should give us all hope  for the future of journalism in this  great democracy the entry is from the  staff of the Eagle Eye student newspaper  at Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School  in parkland Florida which submitted the  obituaries of 17 coaches and classmates  who were killed during a tragic shooting  in their school in February 2018 the  eagle-eyes submission stated that the  student reporters and editors had to  quote put aside our grief and recognize  our role as both survivors journalists  and loved ones of the deceased unquote  these budding journalists remind us of  the media's unwavering commitment to  bearing witness even in the most  wrenching of circumstances in service to  a nation whose very existence depends on  a free and dedicated press there is hope  in their example even as security  threats to journalists are greater than  ever and there is hope even if some  wrongly degrade the media as the enemy  of the very democracy it serves of  course the press will endure because as  the founding fathers knew well there can  be no democracy without it  that is something Rebecca Smith Wendy  Winters robbed hyacinth Gerald Fishman  and John McNamara understood too they  understood it when they went to work  last June at the Capitol Gazette in  Annapolis Maryland and were gunned down  by a madman who opened fire in their  newsroom it is what Jamal could show he  understood as well when he bravely wrote  truth to power at the Washington Post  before being murdered in October 2018  inside the Saudi consulate in Turkey in  all 63 professional journalists  worldwide were killed this year  according to reporters without borders  that is a 15% increase in journalists  who lost their lives for simply trying  to do their jobs and their spirit this  year's winning work reflects yet again a  steely resolve in upholding the highest  principles and ideals of this noble  profession yet what could often seem  like a dark time there do remain places  where the future of journalism seems  bright that's our program for tonight  we'll see you back here next week good  night
 

